Chapter: GopMedium
  cites Works chapter GopDisk
  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Works chapter GopNvme
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 Which medium is mine? On a real machine several controllers carry
 disks at once -- the internal SATA drive on AHCI, the boot stick on
 USB -- and GopDisk's dispatch order would hand every read to the
 internal drive, whose GPT is not ours, whose ESP holds someone
 else's loader, and whose contents no ceremony should touch. The
 boot medium is identified by CONTENT, not by which controller
 answers first: it is the disk whose EFI System Partition carries
 our own CODEX.CDX.

 The probe rides the very seam it configures. GopDisk's selection
 cells steer disk-read-sector; setting them to a candidate makes the
 whole FAT stack above (GPT walk, ESP mount, root-directory lookup)
 read from that candidate, so one gfat-file-size call answers the
 question. A hit leaves the cells locked and every later read --
 storage screens, wake ceremony, identity save -- lands on the
 proven medium. No hit anywhere clears the cells, and the dispatch
 order stands as before, which on a single-disk machine was already
 right.

 USB is probed first: when a stick carrying CODEX.CDX is present it
 is almost certainly what the machine booted from, and probing it
 first keeps a development machine's internal Codex install from
 shadowing the stick in hand. The USB candidate exists only when
 usb-attach published a disk handle -- probing through the connect
 fallback would reset the shared controller and kill the keyboard.

 We say:

Section: Probe

  medium-probe : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  medium-probe (kind) (index) = act
    let s = med-set kind index
    in act
      m <- gfat-mount-esp
      if m.gm-ok == False then False
      else act
        sz <- gfat-file-size (m.gm-vol) "CODEX.CDX"
        sz > 0
      end
    end
  end

Section: Candidates

  medium-try-usb : [Device.Port] Boolean
  medium-try-usb = act
    let p = msc-published
    in if p.md-ok == False then False
    else medium-probe med-kind-usb 0
  end

  medium-ahci-max : Integer = 4

  medium-try-ahci : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  medium-try-ahci (n) = act
    if n >= medium-ahci-max then -1
    else act
      hba <- ahci-find-nth n
      if hba.hba-ok == False then -1
      else act
        ok <- medium-probe med-kind-ahci n
        if ok then n else medium-try-ahci (n + 1)
      end
    end
  end

  medium-try-ide : [Device.Port] Boolean
  medium-try-ide = act
    absent <- ide-absent
    if absent then False
    else medium-probe med-kind-ide 0
  end

 The NVMe candidate connects (and publishes its handle) on first
 touch; a machine without the controller answers the PCI scan with
 nothing and the probe costs one walk of the bus.

  medium-try-nvme : [Device.Port] Boolean
  medium-try-nvme = act
    c <- nvme-disk
    if c.nc-ok == False then False
    else medium-probe med-kind-nvme 0
  end

Section: Selection

 Returns the selected kind, zero when no medium carries CODEX.CDX --
 in which case the cells are cleared and GopDisk's dispatch order
 decides, exactly as before this chapter existed.

  medium-select : [Device.Port] Integer
  medium-select = act
    u <- medium-try-usb
    if u then med-kind-usb
    else act
      a <- medium-try-ahci 0
      if a >= 0 then med-kind-ahci
      else act
        n <- medium-try-nvme
        if n then med-kind-nvme
        else act
          i <- medium-try-ide
          if i then med-kind-ide
          else let c = med-clear 0 in 0
        end
      end
    end
  end
